#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172853} :activity/id #uuid "deaf4c92-c8a0-5ffb-ac01-faeb66f39a39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173121 :tutorial/content "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda.$ \n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/72/72cd99ec8ff51659357f00417203ace84164027e0cb88584cc136f608d86b15a.svg)\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-3x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]+[1] \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.9502.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." :tutorial/id #uuid "3c557f1b-8f84-52d6-823a-8025e70b3114" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10786 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "86f3cc3d-b825-558e-89db-f7ded0fe1e84" :step/math-academy-id 231085 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :question/id #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050018}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435504}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "b8119b3b-4fff-596e-94aa-acf0dc17ccff" :step/math-academy-id 231086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :question/id #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544894}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "3ef50142-79f2-5496-a8b7-805b3150108d" :step/math-academy-id 231089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :question/id #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544872}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "5e0e069a-0219-5cb2-bbfa-c62742dcfbf9" :step/math-academy-id 231092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173122 :tutorial/content "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed. \n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate $\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{ decays/minute}.$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-0.8x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.5507.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1,$ so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big|\\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." :tutorial/id #uuid "50a52f5a-aa3a-5935-9354-a35e097ff394" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10787 :tutorial/title "Modeling Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "48fbdce6-fb42-59ad-9328-65fe0616ba01" :step/math-academy-id 231095 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :question/id #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-16591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544895}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"} :step/id #uuid "a11e0baa-9867-5238-a3a8-1e70022bc1f2" :step/math-academy-id 231096}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172175}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485624} :activity/id #uuid "616b3a6a-2237-5567-8bd3-bad7d041996f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb7dd9eb-cdeb-501a-a394-258b4c0a70a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171448} {:answer/id #uuid "1b166edc-f9ee-54cf-a9dd-16b3169ff4a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171449} {:answer/id #uuid "1e94cdee-8432-5001-bc67-ae18a90f7237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} {:answer/id #uuid "268664a8-6e49-52a3-8c87-9405b615b645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171451} {:answer/id #uuid "339dc04c-c725-5bea-bd34-5df67317f404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7165" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} :answer-field/id #uuid "02f76e3c-1245-5939-a1bc-92a2d23477ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec6131e7-de2b-527c-b4ba-ef95dae870b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96381" :question/problem "At a call center, the time taken for phone calls to be answered varies continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $3$ calls every minute. What is the probability of taking more than $1$ minute to answer the next call? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time until the next call is answered occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, between calls. Then, since there are $3$ calls per minute on average, the rate is $\\lambda =3$ calls per minute.\n\nThe amount of time before the next call can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =3,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next call will occur after $1$ minute or more is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 1) & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.0498.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "362e8628-dbd8-5502-bb8d-0fc43dd4f500" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f02d2d-c10d-5ca4-b6d9-0139b28173a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "13a56b51-6b01-5125-b67e-75c6aab58d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-253892" :question/problem "At a particular helpdesk, customer waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $6$ customers per hour. If a customer has already waited for $6$ minutes, what is the probability that they will need to wait at least $4$ more minutes for their call to be answered? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the customer's waiting time, in minutes. Since the help desk serves $6$ customers per hour on average (or $1$ customer every $10$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ customers per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=6,$ and $t=4.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6+4\\,|\\,X>6) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/5} \\\\  & ≈0.670.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48f1bc6d-2333-56a9-97a8-8077e356fb41" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e33eb3c-cec7-5493-9f32-c984010c4bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0302" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484871} {:answer/id #uuid "275b76d2-5fd6-553c-b3f2-cd785fa8f998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484889} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7f8381-9c80-59cc-9eb2-c3583a9235be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2485" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} {:answer/id #uuid "c29e1a84-9ffd-505b-8a79-6dcd87d514f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4353" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485233} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ea36b6-b0d0-5067-953e-f4ef81d92a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485128} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99fb244-28cb-58fa-857b-0ab86cacbcc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99aa1c5a-eed9-5ae6-b8c6-e2b13db7c0f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96367" :question/problem "Mark runs a hotel near a popular tourist resort. He knows that, on average, people usually book their room $3.5$ days before their trip. By modeling the number of days between the customer's booking and arrival as continuous and assuming that these numbers are independent, determine the probability that the next customer books their room less than $1$ full day in advance. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time interval between the customer's booking and arrival is continuous and the time of different bookings are independent and occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time between the next customer's booking and arrival using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of days between a booking and the customer's arrival. Since customers book their room $3.5=7/2$ days in advance on average, $X$ can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/7,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{7}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer books a room less than $1$ full day in advance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{7}{e}^{-2x/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x/7}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/7}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2485\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81c056b4-eadd-5982-a819-4d1188c88ca4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5baebaba-fb25-532c-a3b0-5a6c14ea2ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.264" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485020} :answer-field/id #uuid "938386cd-7f18-57c1-8aef-c0067eb1a186" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b588bb0d-6d8e-5a2d-ae2c-642f8c01ed06" :question/math-academy-id "q-254586" :question/problem "A fisherman catches fish continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ fish every $15$ minutes. If the fisherman has already been fishing for $30$ minutes without catching any fish, what is the probability that he will need to fish for at least $20$ more minutes before catching his first fish? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since catching fish occurs continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next catch using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next catch, in minutes. Since the fisherman catches $1$ fish every $15$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ fish per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4/3} \\\\  & ≈0.264.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9424cb41-3dfd-528c-948b-f29f36193749"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54cf95-8d93-5f06-addc-038acebc5019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65b508-2845-571b-8454-76c6030fae62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2952" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484842} {:answer/id #uuid "48a6a32b-b9ab-5bec-85a1-8073a3608631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2529" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484974} {:answer/id #uuid "5814f8c1-9cae-53cc-8145-d58f8cc938cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485016} {:answer/id #uuid "668716e1-89c4-559b-bcb0-1a077d43cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2599" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484841} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2f20a1-a54d-5ed7-a2a8-fb23d83de889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b831793d-1ec0-54c8-869c-6f86de445418" :question/math-academy-id "q-132546" :question/problem "Vehicles arrive at a crossing continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the time intervals between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing are continuous and independent, and arrivals occur at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/10$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/10,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing within the next $3$ seconds is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/10}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3/10}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3/10} \\\\  & ≈0.2592\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ce0bc65b-a275-58a3-b6a0-d989f4a9ad43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485616}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484806}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544983} :activity/id #uuid "281bfa6d-174e-50b3-ac57-05c41fca1504" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048224 :topic/id #uuid "89b54209-d10d-5a8f-8829-a2f0c5ee66d4" :topic/math-academy-id 3353} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f15c2a-e5fc-5ca0-bb85-07dbfb14f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.487" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544422} :answer-field/id #uuid "d936cedc-6149-5dd3-984d-e6f7a43f2a04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1e2b523-bf44-5c32-9b95-836b1e8d2e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-254609" :question/problem "While riding on a road, a cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $4$ potholes per hour. If the cyclist has not encountered any potholes in the past $30$ minutes, what is the probability that he will encounter a pothole in the next $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since the cyclist encounters potholes continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next pothole as an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time, in minutes, until the cyclist encounters the next pothole. Since the cyclist encounters, on average, $4$ potholes per hour (or $1$ pothole every $15$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15}$ potholes per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{15}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{15},$ $s=30,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 30+10\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X\\le 10) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/15)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.487.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0cfd11fc-dd9a-5b3c-8a96-28e4560b2041"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa8381d-0308-5944-b790-50b787cd73b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544516} :answer-field/id #uuid "63853831-cd8f-5815-844b-593361f11ad4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d44e0172-2699-5b95-8b6b-c9e2c6a8cdf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-254581" :question/problem "A customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes. If a ticket has been open for $20$ minutes already, what is the probability that it will remain unresolved for at least another $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{5}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "33983c3f-9e33-5e47-b127-51f52627feeb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596675b4-21d2-5ac4-b0a2-4839e83555d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccd3213-2b5a-598b-9a24-9bb6f2190670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8785" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544289} {:answer/id #uuid "d687dca3-0bd3-526a-a4ab-509c47139640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544547} {:answer/id #uuid "daa90ef2-43bc-5481-8ed8-ea54977434e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8892" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544558} {:answer/id #uuid "e7be2a9b-7b4a-533e-9a2f-8958ec2a1213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4dfd42d-e350-5855-be90-2f0889abb592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eddfe563-295a-5298-9f24-7cdb158be9e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132486" :question/problem "A monitor issues a warning signal when an action is needed as part of a production process. Signals occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $0.2$ minutes.  Determine the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the monitor issues a warning signal continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next signal appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between successive signals. Since the monitor issues a warning signal every $0.2=\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =5$ signals per minute.\n\nThe times between two successive signals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-5x}|}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "979ed7bb-94cd-5512-96ff-35ef9c614946" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544974}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5709fe-0354-5d44-b05a-02a21b0da97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} {:answer/id #uuid "d25214fe-4395-53a2-b548-fff04525e163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544534} {:answer/id #uuid "dbff2e28-f33a-5f18-8f52-e274e4c55978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6665" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544561} {:answer/id #uuid "e8872e6f-ba5a-570a-bbd3-ab8d4b6496ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544588} {:answer/id #uuid "fba8fc04-0fa8-577c-bd40-5fbcbbc648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.6565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544456} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c361c4-eefd-5311-9a84-4a9a4e2f34ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bc8f4760-f931-5737-8a9c-5aab0b30c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-132558" :question/problem "The waiting time for one customer to be served at a cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one person served every $4$  minutes. Determine the probability that the next person is served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbb06f0a-ea01-532e-b482-4baad0e7d648" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45bde8d9-4c6e-5ee0-940a-5de511005e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8647" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} {:answer/id #uuid "70f23a81-9ddb-5bb1-b52a-a90210c0f213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8677" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544338} {:answer/id #uuid "75826e41-5f0a-5951-9d1b-962bf48353ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8596" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544348} {:answer/id #uuid "896a0afd-8d66-500b-9100-28316a76192d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8467" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544383} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc1e1f6-fe38-5508-ad8f-639f19a1b5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.8417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544241} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf80934d-3233-573f-8300-6e5e2ddf5899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1455aec3-db48-5efe-b5bf-59b8688bf438" :question/math-academy-id "q-132483" :question/problem "On a webpage, visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $15$ seconds.  Determine the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than $0.5$ minutes.  Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since visitors arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next visitor appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between two consecutive visitor arrivals. Since visitors arrive on average every $15$ seconds, the rate is $\\lambda =60÷15=4$ visitors per minute.\n\nThe time between two consecutive visitor arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =4,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(4).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{-4x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of waiting for the next visitor for less than \n$0.5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-4x}{|}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e2ceb160-b573-55f7-b63a-adf4cc1b5784" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544954}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Exponential Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544072}]}
